+# Congressional routes for DOT’s FY 2006 accountability report
+
+Public derivative of the November 9, 2026 Eastern source review · B129 / PD03–PD05. The Senate recorded a referral for DOT’s FY 2006 Performance and Accountability Report (PAR) under **EC–9158**, on the same day the House recorded **10204**. The Senate receiving committee’s published calendar lists EC–9158. The House Journal supplies an additional locator for 10204. These records provide separate retrieval keys for the cover letters, enclosures and committee files. The underlying letters, enclosures and receipt stamps remain unacquired. [Previous source review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/90847557c230ac903811693bbec5bf89ef5f435f/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-par2006-briefing-and-transmittal-2026-11-09/README.md)
+
+## Preserve the source fields
+
+| Record | Reported fields and actual locator |
+| --- | --- |
+| **House Journal** | `10204.` and `Committee on Government Reform.` The entry describes a letter from the DOT Secretary transmitting the FY 2006 PAR. Physical p. 419, printed p. 1545, lines 108–112. The Dec. 5 date and communications block appear at physical p. 415, printed p. 1541, lines 34 and 56–63. The separately published index appendix lists `¶98.3` and `COMMUNICATIONS` at physical p. 62, printed p. 2260, lines 22–28. [House Journal](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-HJOURNAL-2006/pdf/GPO-HJOURNAL-2006-2-2.pdf#page=419) · [Index appendix](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-HJOURNAL-2006/pdf/GPO-HJOURNAL-2006-3-8.pdf#page=62) |
+| **House Congressional Record** | `10204.`, December 5, 2006, printed H8734. All five extracted referral lines are identical to the House Journal lines, including line-break hyphens. This provides a second publication locator for the same referral. [House Record, physical p. 60, lines 120–124](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2006-12-05/pdf/CREC-2006-12-05.pdf#page=60) |
+| **Senate Congressional Record** | `EC–9158.`, `December 5, 2006`, and `Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs.` The entry describes a communication from the Secretary of Transportation transmitting the FY 2006 PAR. It includes `pursuant to law` and gives no statute number or separate received-on date in this entry. [Senate Record, physical p. 83, printed S11193, lines 1–3 and 45–50](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2006-12-05/pdf/CREC-2006-12-05-senate.pdf#page=83) |
+| **Senate receiving committee’s calendar** | `EC–9158.` under `December 5, 2006—Continued`, attributed to the Secretary of Transportation. The volume cover names the Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs. Its table uses the shorter heading `EXECUTIVE COMMUNICATIONS REFERRED TO COMMITTEE ON GOVERNMENTAL AFFAIRS`. Both labels are preserved as printed. [S. Prt. 109–76, physical p. 163, printed p. 157, lines 1–3 and 40–42](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-LEGCAL-32393/pdf/GPO-LEGCAL-32393.pdf#page=163) |
+
+The dates establish published referral entries. Neither Senate entry for EC–9158 prints a separate arrival date. A neighboring calendar entry, EC–9215, explicitly prints a received-on date, illustrating the difference. Preserve the earlier November 15 report date, November 22 Cino folder-title date and December 5 referral date as separate fields. Matching report titles and fiscal years support a parallel transmittal lead. Actual signed covers, version/enclosure lists and accession or intake records could establish what each committee received. The publications leave that version relationship unresolved. [Calendar date control, printed p. 157, lines 63–67](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-LEGCAL-32393/pdf/GPO-LEGCAL-32393.pdf#page=163) · [Earlier date records](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/90847557c230ac903811693bbec5bf89ef5f435f/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-par2006-briefing-and-transmittal-2026-11-09/README.md)
+
+## Retrieval controls
+
+- **The number alone can point to an unrelated item.** House Journal item `9158.` in the September 6 proceedings concerns an acquisition rule and is separate from Senate `EC–9158.`. Senate `EC–9134.` on December 5 also came from the Secretary of Transportation, but concerns FY 2004 highway and safety construction funds and goes to Commerce, Science, and Transportation. Use chamber, Congress/session, number, subject, referral date and committee together. Keep House 3550 on its own trail. [House Journal, physical and printed p. 1107, lines 115–126](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-HJOURNAL-2006/pdf/GPO-HJOURNAL-2006-2-1.pdf#page=1107) · [Senate EC–9134, physical p. 82, lines 125–135](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2006-12-05/pdf/CREC-2006-12-05-senate.pdf#page=82)
+- **House committee locator.** The NARA standing-committee list groups the former Committee on Government Reform with the successor committee under catalog link 10518159. That is an organization-level lead. NARA describes separate preliminary-inventory coverage through the 87th House and 93rd Senate Congresses and distinguishes later inventories and box lists. The bounded RG 233 export listing returned eight object keys and explicitly indicates truncation. Those keys alone do not identify an accession, committee box or item. [NARA House list, raw HTML lines 484–485](https://www.archives.gov/legislative/research/browse/house-standing.html) · [NARA finding-aid guide, lines 408–412](https://www.archives.gov/legislative/research/getting-started/whats-not-online.html)
+- **Separate public-access triggers.** NARA describes ordinary House and Senate access periods of 30 and 20 years respectively. Its House Rule VII transcription is labeled **117th Congress**, with separate treatment of previously public records, special orders and categories subject to a longer period. Senate Resolution 474 §2 distinguishes its twenty-year default from specified fifty-year records and allows different committee conditions. Treat the Senate age threshold as a potential trigger to revisit the catalog. The date of existence, transfer, category, conditions and actual availability of either packet require item-level evidence. [NARA access summary, raw HTML lines 344–350](https://www.archives.gov/legislative/research/rules-of-access.html) · [House Rule VII, lines 349–373](https://www.archives.gov/legislative/research/house-rule-vii.html) · [Senate Resolution 474, lines 340–355](https://www.archives.gov/legislative/research/senate-resolution-474.html)
+
+## Verification and open work
+
+Thirteen public response bodies and the held House daily PDF matched their recorded hashes. Twenty-five PDF text anchors, fifteen raw HTML anchors, five identical House referral lines and four selected page faces were checked. The bounded case-key screen covered 58 PDF text pages. The separately acquired House activity-report HTML also had no hit on the two referral identifiers or the exact case keys. These checks leave the rest of the print volumes, archive objects beyond the listing, native letters and enclosure contents outside scope.
+
+**TODO:** Obtain the House 10204 and Senate EC–9158 signed covers, received copies or delivery records, and attachment or version identifiers. On the DOT side, seek the outgoing control entry for each recipient. Compare those records with the Cino folder only when its contents are available. Keep the case-specific corrected May 2003 QFR, receipt, signed personnel decision and recommendation outcomes open under their existing keys. An annual-report transmittal answers a different question from implementation or delivery of the corrected answer. [Case recommendations, report physical pp. 12–13](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12) · [Exact source and verification ledger](SOURCE-PINS.json)
